patches/browser-pthread-pool.mjs applies exact, SHA-256-guarded changes to
PThread.getNewWorker in lite4mariadb.js. In browsers an exhausted pool returns
undefined instead of allocating an unloaded Worker. The caller already maps
that result to pthread_create EAGAIN. This matches Emscripten's supported
PTHREAD_POOL_SIZE_STRICT=2 behavior, retaining dynamic worker creation in Node.

The upstream build uses PTHREAD_POOL_SIZE=16 and PTHREAD_POOL_SIZE_STRICT=0.
MariaDB's internal task pool can request additional workers while synchronous
native shutdown waits for tasks to finish. Browser workers created at that point
cannot initialize until the blocked creator returns to its event loop, causing
a shutdown deadlock. Returning EAGAIN lets MariaDB's task pool undo the failed
worker allocation and continue using its initialized workers.

Reference: Emscripten src/lib/libpthread.js, PThread.getNewWorker
https://github.com/emscripten-core/emscripten/blob/6.0.6/src/lib/libpthread.js

The WebAssembly binary, SQL engine, native PDO source, upstream npm package,
and lockfile package-integrity hash are not modified. joomengine-patches.json
and the release manifest record the original and transformed glue SHA-256
values and the unchanged WASM SHA-256. Ship this notice with the engine.

Reproduce the transformation from the original upstream npm package without
installing any dependencies:
node patches/browser-pthread-pool.mjs upstream/lite4mariadb.js prepared/lite4mariadb.js

This self-contained transformation source ships in both release archives.

The browser-only logical CPU scheduling budget is capped at four. InnoDB's
native generic pool targets twice the reported concurrency; the fixed 16-worker
browser budget must also serve its background services. The Node core count is
unchanged. This scheduling cap avoids asking the strict browser pool to create
workers beyond its capacity and does not change the upstream WASM binary.
Reference: tpool/tpool_generic.cc, thread_pool_generic::set_concurrency at
